e, G7 and K11, are empty, and the positions extending past those (F6 and L12) are also clear. White has stones at K8, J9, and B11, but none of these pose an immediate lethal threat (like a 4-in-a-row) or block Black\'s main line.\n\nReason: The most effective move is to extend the diagonal line of three into a line of four. By playing at G7 (or K11), Black creates an "Open 4" (four stones in a row with open spaces on both ends). This is a guaranteed win because the opponent can only block one end on their next turn. If White blocks F6, Black wins at K11. If White blocks K11, Black wins at F6. Therefore, G7 is a winning move.\n\nAnswer: { "col": "G", "row": 7 }'}
